  <content>
    <section class="practice">
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
        <exercise id="_0e64d705-1a60-4342-b809-5c7af161812b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph of a quadratic function \(f(x)\). What is the minimum value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
            <para>Enter the minimum value of \(f(x)\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The minimum value of \(f(x)\) is 0.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.11.2 and 9.11.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0. The minimum value of \(f(x)\) is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9b01f8bf-8316-425b-8486-d01357b4d5f6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Explain how we can tell from the expression \((x+1)^2−4\) that -4 is the minimum value of \(f\) rather than the maximum value.</para>
            <para>The graph that represents \(f(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) has its vertex at \((-1,-4)\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The constant is always the minimum value.</para>
            <para>The expression \((x+1)^2\) is a squared expression, so its value will always be positive or zero.</para>
            <para>We cannot tell from the expression; we have to graph it.</para>
            <para>The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.11.2 and 9.11.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1e493ddb-22a6-46bd-b07e-12c4b0e1e2a7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the \(x\)-coordinate.</para>
            <para>\(g(x)=(x−5)^2+6\) defines a quadratic function. Find the vertex of the graph of the function. Then, state whether the vertex corresponds to the maximum or the minimum value of the function.</para>
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is 5.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.11.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 5. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is 5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
